key_points:
Conditional recommendations favor testing in three family-history scenarios:
- relatives of people with antithrombin, protein C or protein S deficiency, when considering prophylaxis for minor provoking factors or deciding on hormonal contraceptives/hormone replacement therapy;
- pregnant women with a family history of high-risk thrombophilia;
- cancer patients at low or intermediate thrombosis risk with a family history of VTE.

Per IDSA 2018, do treat these groups:
  - all hospitalized patients;
  - outpatients with progressive illness, regardless of illness duration;
  - high-risk outpatients, including those with chronic conditions or immunocompromise;
  - children <2 years and adults >65 years;
  - pregnant women and those <2 weeks postpartum.

If there is no improvement in clinical findings or fever curve after 36 hours, or orbital signs are new or worsening:
  - consult ID;
  - consider repeat imaging;
  - reconsider surgical management in discussion with ophthalmology and ENT attendings.

High-risk conditions strongly associated with severe disease include:
- moderate or severe immunocompromise (for example, steroids ≥20 mg/day or ≥2 mg/kg/day for ≥14 days, CD4 <300 or <15%, SOT within 3 months);
- severe heart disease;
- BMI ≥30 or ≥95th percentile;
- dependence on respiratory technology;
- severe neurologic or genetic disability;
- severe asthma or lung disease requiring ≥2 inhaled or ≥1 systemic medications daily.
Sickle cell disease, poorly controlled diabetes, and prematurity with age ≤2 years warrant case-by-case consideration.

Work-up for admitted patients:
- CBC with diff, CMP, and PT if high-risk or on supplemental oxygen;
- CXR if on oxygen or persistently tachycardic despite IVF;
- respiratory viral panel (FARVPP), without waiting for results.
Give judicious IVF, antibiotics per NCH standards, and respiratory support as needed.

The standard care backbone in the trial was:
- restricted crystalloid;
- PRBC:FFP at 1:1 to 2:1;
- TXA 1 g load within 3 hours of injury, then 1 g over 8 hours;
- early hemorrhage control;
- fibrinogen concentrate if fibrinogen <1.5 g/L or viscoelastic testing showed a deficit;
- platelets to keep the count >50 x10^9/L.
